  "query_preview": "..\nThrough Tuesday...\n\nOur weather over the next week will be dictated largely by the\ndirty upper level low sitting to our west over the PNW. The \nenergy/vorticity with this feature is not neatly organized and \nthus the location will wobble slightly over the next few days \nwhich will sometimes push into our CWA, while other times may \nretrograde slightly. When the low is farther west much of the CWA,\nespecially our east, will be under the influence of the ridge \nimpacting the central Great Plains. \n\nThe first of these pushes of energy will impact primarily western\nand northern Montana later today. While most of the energy will be\ncloser towards the low in NW MT and Western AB, a trailing area of\nf-gen will sag through our entire CWA. Ahead of this front there\nwill be some gusty winds in the foothills of south-centeral\nMontana, esepcailly in gap prone areas such as Livingston an Big\nTimber. This wind, combined with the low RH values will produce\nelevated fire weather concerns, with an emphasis on locations\noutside of the higher elevations. \n\nAs the vort max moves to our NW and the front pushes through our\narea showers and thunderstorms will initiate off of the mountains and\nmove towards the NE. These storms should remain fairly benign to \nstart before they get into a more supportive environment. While \nany storm could produce gusty winds, the severe wind gusts are \nmost likely in our NW zones near Harlowton. Soundings here show a\nclassic inverted-v type environment in addition to 30 to 40 kt \nwinds in the 700-500mb layer which could be forced down in \nstronger convection. As the storms that initiated off of the \nBighorns move to the NE they will encounter a better environment \nonce they get through Rosebud Co. Low level stratus this morning \nwas indicative of good moisture advection out of the plains into\nfar eastern MT and now the obs are showing this with dew points \nat KMLS in the mid 50s F and at KBHK in the low 60s F. These SE \nwinds at the surface will remain as the storms move towards the \narea late this afternoon into the evening creating good low level \nshear with veering winds through the lowest 2km AGL with 90 \ndegrees of directional sheer. ML CAPE around 2000 J/Kg near Baker \nat 23z per the RAP combined with a good distribution of this \nenergy throughout the column should support good updraft growth; \nwhen combining that with 40kts of 0-8km shear the updrafts should \nbe able to support supercell type storms easily. Aside from the \nwinds and heavy rain that come standard with supercells, the main \nthreat bear Baker, MT will be large hail. With ~20kts of speed \nshear in the -10C to -30C layer, the tilt of the updrafts wil be \nable to support good hydrometer size sorting; combined with the \nwell distributed CAPE, large hail is possible. I would not be \nsurprised to see the strongest storm of the evening even drop a \nfew golf ball sized stones.\n\nLooking at Thursday, there has been a slight decrease in overall \ndynamics across the region. 700mb winds peak in the morning hours \nbefore weakening to 20-30kts during peak daytime heating hours. \nWith the lack of dynamics and forcing, widespread precipitation \nand clouds is not expected which will also keep min RH values low.\nIt is worth noting though that the RH values have come up just a \nfew percent in our far western zones with the latest model runs. \nThis is likely due to the weaker downslope winds. All that being \nsaid, there is still a chance for locally critical fire weather\nacross the western half of our forecast area Thursday. \n\nAs we head into the weekend there is still some uncertainty \naround the forecast. There is better agreement that the parent \nlow will slowly move eastwards starting Friday putting the \nwestern 1/3 of the CWA under an area of CVA and SW flow. This \ncould bring daily chances for showers and storms for locations \nwest of Billings if it verifies. Additionally, it could also bring\nperiods of breezy conditions across the region. On Friday the \nforecast winds have come up a bit for a lot of the area. Due to \nthe CVA ahead of the shortwave as well as warm temperatures, \nthere will be a SFC low that develops roughly over Billings which\nshould help enhance winds to its east. This would result in \nelevated fire weather across our southern and eastern zones when \nyou combine highs in the 90s and Min RH values in the teens. \n\nEarly next week it looks like another trough could dig off of the\nCA coast which would help finally push our initial shortwave\nthrough and out of our area. This could result in a good chance of\nprecipitation and a notable cooldown. This new trough would then \ntake over as our primary weather maker. WMR",
